WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.380
Your keypad detector runs live:

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.300
every block
of two hundred fifty-six samples,

3
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.700
it matches eight test wheels,

4
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.520
its bins, one per keypad tone,

5
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.920
against a twelve percent bar.

6
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.400
Then two complaints arrive.

7
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.840
Loud presses of four get thrown out,

8
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.960
since the seven's row clears the bar too.

9
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.840
And faint rural keys are never heard.

10
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.700
Lower the bar to six percent?

11
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.400
The rural keys come through,

12
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.000
and loud keys are thrown out instead.

13
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.120
Why does a loud four light up seven's bin?

14
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:25.690
Shrink it to a napkin:

15
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.790
eight samples,
bins a thousand hertz apart.

16
00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.510
A thousand hertz tone sits on its bin:
one hundred percent,

17
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.070
and zero everywhere else.

18
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.810
Move it halfway, to fifteen hundred:

19
00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:35.290
sixty and seventy in the nearest bins,

20
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.790
and every bin catches something.

21
00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:37.710
That's leakage.

22
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.070
A thousand hertz makes
exactly one turn in the block.

23
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:42.870
Fifteen hundred stops halfway round:

24
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.710
the block ends partway through a turn.

25
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.140
The test wheel turns each arrow
back and lays them tip to tail.

26
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.160
On the bin,
they freeze and pile up straight.

27
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.240
Off it, the leftover arrow keeps turning,

28
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.140
half a turn over the block,
so the chain fans out: sixty-four percent.

29
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.260
The mirror's short chain
nudges it to sixty.

30
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.020
Only whole turns close a chain.

31
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.280
Slide the tone: the matches trace
one curve: lesson ten's tall hump,

32
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.920
the main lobe, one bin each side.

33
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.100
Then side lobes:
the first reaches twenty-two percent,

34
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.540
and the rest shrink slowly.

35
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.510
On the keypad, the seven's bin sits
on a side lobe of seven seventy.

36
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.670
Depending on where each
tone starts in the block,

37
00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.990
a loud four leaks about nine
to fifteen percent there,

38
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.710
and a faint key can score under eight.

39
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.450
A faint key's real tone is weaker
than a loud key's leak. No bar fits.

40
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.880
The trouble is the edges:
the block cuts a turn short,

41
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.280
and the end arrows count in full.

42
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.580
Lesson ten cured a sharp cut with a taper.

43
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.700
Multiply the block by the Hann window,

44
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.000
Hamming's cousin,
fading to zero at both ends.

45
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.160
The end arrows shrink,

46
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.060
and the side lobe falls from twenty-two
percent to two point seven.

47
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.340
Now the faintest key
scores nine point two,

48
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.680
and a bar at five percent
decodes every press.

49
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.290
Why not a shorter block?

50
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.570
At one twenty-eight, a loud one leaks
sixty-one percent into the four's bin:

51
00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.710
the taper bought low side lobes
with a main lobe twice as wide.

52
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.810
Longer isn't the rule:
two twenty-four fails, two oh eight works.

53
00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.590
What counts is where the bins land.

54
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.310
With loud keys ten times the faint,

55
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.610
every neighbouring tone must land
at least about one point seven five bins

56
00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:05.870
from the bin you read.

57
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.030
Two fifty-six gives two point two six.

58
00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:10.210
Your turn, in the lab:

59
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.210
a call centre joins, twice as loud.

60
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.370
Choose window, length and bar until
all thirty-two presses decode.

61
00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.670
Next ticket: a tip line shifts
every call up three hundred hertz,

62
00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.210
and a second voice mumbles underneath.
